[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,7.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(8.6,0);
\foreach \y in {0.3,0.6,0.9,1.2,1.8,2.1,2.4,2.7,3.3,3.6,3.9,4.2,4.8,5.1,5.4,5.7} {\draw (-0.1,\y)--(0,\y);}
\foreach \y/\lbl in {0/0,1.5/10,3.0/20,4.5/30,6.0/40} {\draw (-0.18,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\y) {$\lbl$};}
\draw (0.9,0) rectangle (2.0,3.9);
\draw (2.7,0) rectangle (3.8,6.0);
\draw (4.5,0) rectangle (5.6,4.8);
\draw (6.3,0) rectangle (7.4,2.7);
\foreach \x/\lbl in {1.45/Emu,3.25/Koala,5.05/Wombat,6.85/Dingo} {\node[below,font=\tiny] at (\x,0) {\lbl};}
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-1.1,3.0) {Entries};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.15,-1.0) {House};
\node[font=\small] at (4.15,7.1) {Entries by house};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The column graph shows how many students from each house entered events at a school carnival. Which conclusion does the graph support?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,7.4);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(8.4,0);
\foreach \y in {0.5,1.5,2.5,3.5,4.5,5.5} {\draw (-0.1,\y)--(0,\y);}
\foreach \y/\lbl in {0/0,1/50,2/100,3/150,4/200,5/250,6/300} {\draw (-0.18,\y)--(0,\y); \node[left,font=\scriptsize] at (-0.2,\y) {$\lbl$};}
\foreach \x/\lbl in {1/1,2.2/2,3.4/3,4.6/4,5.8/5,7.0/6} {\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (\x,0) {\lbl};}
\draw[thick] (1,2.5)--(2.2,4.0)--(3.4,3.5)--(4.6,5.0)--(5.8,4.5)--(7.0,6.0);
\foreach \p in {(1,2.5),(2.2,4.0),(3.4,3.5),(4.6,5.0),(5.8,4.5),(7.0,6.0)} {\fill \p circle (2.5pt);}
\node[rotate=90,font=\scriptsize] at (-1.9,3.0) {Books};
\node[below,font=\scriptsize] at (4.0,-1.0) {Week};
\node[font=\small] at (4.0,7.1) {Books borrowed};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The line graph shows how many books were borrowed from a school library in each of six weeks. The library put up new displays during the term. Which conclusion does the graph support?
